extends /views/templates/layouts/_base

append variables
	- pagetitle = "Features | Legends"
	- pagedescription = "Legends in Tablechart: the names of lines or slices, in a row under the chart."

block content
	.lane
		.container
			.page-header
				h1 Legends
				p.lead The names of the lines or slices, in a row under the chart.

	+section("Legend for several lines")
		p On a line chart with several lines, the names are shown at the end of the lines. If you want them in a legend under the chart instead, add #[code data-chart-legend] to the figure. Each name has a short sample of its line.

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure(data-chart="line" data-chart-legend)
				figcaption: b Apples per tree
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Year
							th North
							th South
							th East
					tbody
						each row in [['2016', '41', '30', '22'], ['2019', '58', '39', '35'], ['2022', '77', '52', '41'], ['2025', '105', '61', '70']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]
								td= row[3]

		p If you want to use a legend for every chart, set the #[code legend] option. Then #[code data-chart-legend="false"] turns it off for one chart.

		+codeblock('JS').
			init({ legend: true });

	+section("Area, column and bar legends")
		p In an area chart, #[code data-chart-legend] works the same way. Each name has a square of the colour of its area.
		p A grouped or stacked column or bar chart always has a legend under the chart, with a square for each name.

	+section("Donut legend")
		p A donut chart always has a legend under the ring. See #[a(href=url('/types/donut.html')) Donut].

	+section("Turn series off and on")
		p With #[code data-chart-legend="toggle"], each name in the legend is a button, underlined as a link is. If you click a name, or press Enter or Space on it, its series is turned off, and the name gets a line through it. If you click it again, the series is turned on again. The last series that is on stays on.

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure(data-chart="column" data-chart-legend="toggle")
				figcaption
					b Apples sold
					br
					span thousands
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Month
							th Farm shop
							th Market
							th Online
					tbody
						each row in [['Jan', '34', '41', '120'], ['Feb', '38', '45', '140'], ['Mar', '42', '53', '180'], ['Apr', '51', '49', '210']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]
								td= row[3]

		p If a series is turned off, the chart is rendered again for the series that are left:
		ul
			li The scale fits the values that are left, so small values get more room.
			li In a group, the other columns or bars close up. In a stack, the total is the sum of the parts that are left.
			li The numbers are placed again, so a number that had no room can come back.
			li The tooltip shows only the series that are on.
		p The columns, lines, points and numbers that stay move to their new place. What is new fades in, and what is gone fades out. If the chart has no animation, or the reader asked for less motion, the chart changes at once.
		p The table does not change, so a screen reader still reads all values. Toggles work on line, area, column and bar charts with several value columns, and on a combo chart. If you want toggles on every chart with a legend, set the #[code legend] option to #[code "toggle"].

		+codeblock('JS').
			init({ legend: "toggle" });

		p If a name has focus from the keyboard, it gets a focus ring. If the page has its own focus style for #[code :focus-visible], the names use that style. If not, #[code --tablechart-legend-focus-color], #[code --tablechart-legend-focus-width] and #[code --tablechart-legend-focus-offset] set the colour, width and space of the ring.

	+section("Toggles with two value axes")
		p On a chart with #[a(href=url('/types/combo.html#two-value-axes')) two value axes], each axis has a scale for the series that are on at its side. If every series of one axis is turned off, the chart has one value axis, on the side of the series that are left. If you turn one of them on again, the second axis comes back.
		p In this example, rainfall and irrigation use the axis on the left, and temperature the axis on the right. If you turn off Temperature, the chart has one axis, on the left. If you turn off Rainfall and Irrigation, the temperature line keeps its axis on the right.

		+chart(null, '1,5')
			figure(data-chart="column" data-chart-grid data-chart-legend="toggle")
				figcaption
					b Water at the orchard
					br
					span per month
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Month
							th Rainfall (mm)
							th Irrigation (mm)
							th(data-chart="line" data-axis="right") Temperature (°C)
					tbody
						each row in [['Apr', '41', '12', '9.9'], ['May', '58', '18', '13.4'], ['Jun', '65', '30', '16.1'], ['Jul', '74', '42', '18.3'], ['Aug', '80', '35', '18.0'], ['Sep', '76', '15', '15.0']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]
								td= row[3]

	+section("Style the legend")
		p A legend is as wide as its items. These variables set its background, border, padding and corners. By default, there are none.

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure(data-chart="line" data-chart-legend style="--tablechart-legend-border: 1px solid currentColor; --tablechart-legend-padding: 0.3em 0.8em; --tablechart-legend-radius: 0.4em")
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Year
							th North
							th South
					tbody
						each row in [['2016', '41', '30'], ['2019', '58', '39'], ['2022', '77', '52'], ['2025', '105', '61']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]

		table
			thead
				tr
					th Variable
					th Sets
			tbody
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-legend-surface
					td The background
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-legend-border
					td The border
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-legend-padding
					td The space inside the border
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-legend-radius
					td The corners
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-legend-gap
					td The space above the legend of a line chart
